<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.vnx.fleet-role-drift</string>
  <!--
    absence-is-loud, punt 3: fleet_role_drift.py measures how far the fleet has
    fallen behind the canonical T0 role, and its write-state flag records the
    verdict as the fleet_role_drift health beacon. Nothing ever invoked it, so
    the beacon did not exist and the SessionStart digest read the component as
    absent. This job is the driver, nothing else: it runs the measurement on a
    cadence. It does not sync roles and does not touch any project checkout
    (the tool is read-only apart from that one beacon).

    Interval: every 6h (21600s), the same reasoning as com.vnx.ledger-health.
    The tool writes its beacon with expected_interval_seconds=86400, and
    health_beacon.all_beacons() reads a beacon whose age exceeds that as
    "stale". A StartInterval at or above 86400 would make the beacon stale by
    construction. 21600s gives 4 runs a day: even 3 consecutive missed runs
    (an 18h gap) still land inside the window with margin. RunAtLoad makes the
    first run happen on install, so the beacon exists right away instead of
    after the first interval.

    A non-zero exit is expected and normal, not a job failure. Exit 1 means at
    least one project is behind the canon: that is the finding the tool exists
    to record, and it is what launchctl list shows as the last exit status
    whenever the fleet is behind (measured 2026-09-23: 4 of 4 projects behind).
    The beacon carries the same verdict as status fail. Exit 2 means the meter
    could not measure (canon absent, registry unreadable) and no beacon is
    written, so it ages out to stale on its own. Do not wire this job's exit
    code into anything that fails a build.

    The store is NOT pinned by path. The job identifies its project through
    VNX_PROJECT_ID (below); the ambient resolver (vnx_paths.resolve_paths())
    derives the data and state directories from that id, and the beacon lands
    in that project's health directory. A consumer installing this template
    for their own project MUST set VNX_PROJECT_ID to their project id;
    reload_plist.sh substitutes the placeholder from its second argument,
    then from VNX_PROJECT_ID, then from the .vnx-project-id marker.
    Hardcoding a store path here would make a copied template write the
    beacon of the original project (same failure mode com.vnx.ledger-health
    already documents).

    vnx init is not wired to install this job. Install it manually:
      bash scripts/launchd/reload_plist.sh com.vnx.fleet-role-drift
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>cd ${VNX_HOME} &amp;&amp; exec python3 scripts/fleet_role_drift.py --write-state</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>21600</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-fleet-role-drift.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-fleet-role-drift.err</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>VNX_HOME</key>
    <string>${VNX_HOME}</string>
    <key>VNX_PROJECT_ID</key>
    <string>${VNX_PROJECT_ID}</string>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
